<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">First Bulgarian Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">681–1018</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Danubian Bulgaria</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The First Bulgarian Empire was a medieval state founded around 681 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgar</a> khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asparuh-of-bulgaria">Asparukh</a> on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. It became a leading Balkan power, adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> under Boris I in the 860s and, under Simeon I (893–927), became a centre of Slavonic literature written in Glagolitic and Cyrillic. Weakened by the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev">Sviatoslav I of Kiev</a> in 968–971 and by Byzantine wars, it was conquered by the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii">Basil II</a> by 1018.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-bulgarian-empire.jpg" alt="First Bulgarian Empire" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Iamnhaz · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:First_Bulgarian_Empire_Flag_Krum_Manasses_Chronicle_Render.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
